<Base title="About"><header class="page-head"><p class="eyebrow">Yes, I kept the weird versions</p><h1>About the project</h1><p class="lead">They Forgot the Alamo is my unfinished, low-poly browser game about a man who wakes up convinced he's Davy Crockett. This is where I'm keeping track of how it's coming together.</p></header><section class="section"><h2>Where things stand</h2><p>Right now, the playable build is one room. You can move around, aim, wreck some of it, fiddle with the PS1-style graphics, and swap into a raccoon's point of view. It is very much a prototype. The premise is getting ahead of the campaign at the moment.</p><p>I'm piecing the notes together from the commits, screenshots, model tests, and bits of project history I still have. When I rebuild an old version to get a screenshot, I'll say so. I don't want a nice-looking archive pretending my file history is tidier than it is.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Why keep the rough stuff?</h2><p>Because the failed models, strange settings, and awkward poses are part of how I got here. The oldest saved game version I can verify is from September 29, 2026. That's the oldest receipt I have, not necessarily the day I first started thinking about the game.</p></section></Base>
